<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Zealova Live Activity</string>
	<!--
		`simctl install` used to fail on this extension with
		"Invalid placeholder attributes / Failed to create app extension
		placeholder for …FitWizLiveActivityExtension.appex", which blocked
		every simulator install of the app. There were TWO independent
		causes, both fixed 2026-07-31 (E2E register row 155). An earlier
		attempt fixed neither and its comment asserted the opposite of what
		the installer actually does, so the reasoning is recorded in full.

		1. The built .appex carried NO CFBundleShortVersionString and NO
		   CFBundleVersion. This target sets GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES,
		   so Xcode's generated plist overwrites whatever is written here —
		   and it takes the versions from MARKETING_VERSION /
		   CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION, which are "$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NAME)" /
		   "$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)". Those variables live in
		   Flutter/Generated.xcconfig, which only the Runner target used to
		   include, so here they resolved EMPTY and Xcode dropped the keys.
		   Writing literal versions in THIS file therefore cannot work — the
		   generated plist wins. The fix is upstream: the target now has
		   Flutter/AppExtension.xcconfig as its base configuration, which
		   supplies both variables, so the versions track pubspec.yaml and
		   cannot drift from the host app (Apple requires the appex
		   CFBundleShortVersionString to match the app's).

		2. NSExtensionPrincipalClass must NOT be declared at all. WidgetKit
		   extensions find their principal bundle via @main, and the
		   installer rejects the key outright — "defines either an
		   NSExtensionMainStoryboard or NSExtensionPrincipalClass key, which
		   is not allowed for the extension point
		   com.apple.widgetkit-extension" — even when the value is an empty
		   string. It had been present-but-empty on the belief that iOS 26
		   required it; it is the reason install still failed after (1).

		NSExtensionAttributes stays as an explicit empty dict: harmless, and
		the placeholder validator wants the key present.
	-->
	<key>NSExtension</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSExtensionAttributes</key>
		<dict/>
		<key>NSExtensionPointIdentifier</key>
		<string>com.apple.widgetkit-extension</string>
	</dict>
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
